---
import CaseContents from "./CaseContents.astro";
---
<article class="grounded-case">
  <header class="intro">
    <div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Grounded</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Grounded</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">10× engagement</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div>
    <div class="intro__grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">UX Strategy &amp; Behavioral Design</p><h1>Grounded<br /><span class="heading-accent">Climate</span><br />Solutions</h1></div>
      <p class="lede">Grounded connects scientists, innovators, and communities around climate solutions. Working with Pocket Made as Senior UX Strategist, I helped redesign its website around readable stories, clear event registration, and a content system the team could manage in WordPress.</p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Research"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Visual language"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Content system"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} />

  <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/grounded_hero.webp" alt="A hiker walking on a snow-covered mountain ridge" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>


  <section class="grounded-detail" aria-labelledby="grounded-scope">
    <div><p class="grounded-detail__label">The brief</p><h2 id="grounded-scope">Support the audience and the publishing team.</h2></div>
    <div class="grounded-detail__copy"><p>The previous platform struggled with engagement and event registration. Routine content changes also depended on developers. The redesign needed to help visitors find useful material and give a small team a manageable publishing workflow.</p><p>My work covered UX strategy, the visual design system, content architecture, and front-end WordPress development. Content strategy was developed with the Grounded team, connecting the site’s structure to the stories and events they needed to publish.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="research" aria-labelledby="research-title" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1">
    <div class="section-heading"><p>01 / Research</p><h2 id="research-title">Research &amp; Behavioral<br />Design Framework</h2></div>
    <div class="research__copy"><p>I reviewed published behavioral research on how climate communication frames problems and possible responses. The research informed a design hypothesis: acknowledge the scale of the challenge, then give readers a specific way to learn, participate, or connect. This was a literature review, not original audience research.</p><p>The team audited 10 environmental nonprofit websites and looked at editorial references including TED and science journalism. I used those references to explore how article hierarchy, speaker profiles, and event information could make a complex subject easier to navigate.</p></div>
    <div class="research__art"><figure><img src="/images/grounded_01.webp" alt="Grounded color system with icy neutrals, misty greens, and calm blues" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/grounded_02.webp" alt="Responsive design process artifact" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/grounded_03.webp" alt="Grounded website and campaign design shown in a desktop mockup" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div>
  </section>

  <section class="language" aria-labelledby="language-title" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1">
    <div class="language__head"><p>02 / Visual language</p><h2 id="language-title"><span class="language-title-line">A Visual Language for</span><span class="heading-accent language-title-line">Climate Solutions</span></h2></div>
    <div class="language__grid"><div class="language__copy"><p>I built the visual direction around icy neutrals, misty greens, and calm blues. Typography gave long-form content a clear reading order. Photography focused on people, ecosystems, and exploration, supporting the stories without making every page feel like an emergency appeal.</p><p>The aim was to make room for attention and curiosity while keeping the subject serious. Slow animation and parallax added movement to the editorial layouts. These were design choices to support the tone, not evidence that the interface changed a reader’s emotional state.</p></div><figure class="portrait portrait--explorer"><img src="/images/grounded_04.webp" alt="Winter explorer in Grounded’s solution-oriented photography system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure class="portrait portrait--julia"><img src="/images/grounded_julia.webp" alt="Grounded speaker profile component" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure class="portrait portrait--bear"><img src="/images/grounded_05.webp" alt="Polar bear content illustration in Grounded’s visual language" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div>
  </section>

  <section class="architecture" aria-labelledby="architecture-title" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1">
    <div class="architecture__title"><p>03 / A system for action</p><h2 id="architecture-title">A Content System the Team Could Maintain</h2></div>
    <figure><img src="/images/grounded_web_01.webp" alt="Grounded website shown across desktop and mobile layouts" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
    <div class="architecture__copy"><p>I organized the platform around research stories, solutionist profiles, and live events. A three-level navigation structure and consistent icons helped distinguish those content types. Reusable components gave each page a recognizable structure while accommodating different stories and speakers.</p><p>The WordPress work included a countdown and registration experience for events. The route from learning about a session to signing up was part of the content architecture, so event details and participation sat within the same experience.</p></div>
  </section>


  <section class="grounded-detail" aria-labelledby="grounded-handoff">
    <div><p class="grounded-detail__label">Documentation &amp; handoff</p><h2 id="grounded-handoff">Make the next update straightforward.</h2></div>
    <div class="grounded-detail__copy"><p>I documented the components for nontechnical editors and supplied editorial guidance for using them consistently. The handoff covered the system as a working publishing tool, including how new stories, profiles, and events could fit the existing layouts.</p><p>This made maintainability part of the UX brief. A clear visitor experience depended on the team being able to keep its content current after the initial launch.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="results" aria-labelledby="results-title" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1">
    <div><p>04 / Results</p><h2 id="results-title">Results &amp;<br />Community Impact</h2></div>
    <div class="results__facts"><div><strong>300%</strong><span>increase in webinar registrations</span></div><div><strong>10x</strong><span>increase in content engagement</span></div><div><strong>&lt;3s</strong><span>reported page load time</span></div></div>
    <div class="results__copy"><p>The original project account reports a 300% increase in webinar registrations during the first quarter after launch and a tenfold increase in content engagement compared with the previous site. These are reported project outcomes; the available record does not isolate the effect of individual design changes.</p><p>The same account records page loads below three seconds and a team handoff completed within two weeks without support requests. The practical goal was a site that supported ongoing publishing and events without making routine updates dependent on a developer.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="grounded-detail" aria-labelledby="grounded-reflection">
    <div><p class="grounded-detail__label">Reflection</p><h2 id="grounded-reflection">Test the framing with the audience earlier.</h2></div>
    <div class="grounded-detail__copy"><p>I would bring qualitative audience research into the project before committing to the visual system. Published research gave the team a starting point, but it could not tell us how Grounded’s own readers would interpret a particular story, image, or invitation to participate.</p><p>Early interviews and prototype sessions would help test those assumptions. I would focus on whether people could explain the organization’s purpose, find an event relevant to them, and understand the next step toward attending.</p></div>
  </section>
</article>

